 <h2 id="comparison">Fitness and nutrition app comparison</h2>
 <h3>Centr</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> people who want workouts, meals and mindfulness presented as one lifestyle program.</p><p><strong>Consider:</strong> whether you prefer a curated program or detailed food logging. Meal plans and recipes are different from tracking everything you eat.</p>
 <h3>FitOn</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> users who value guided classes and workout variety and may want premium meal planning and recipes.</p><p><strong>Consider:</strong> which nutrition features require the premium tier and whether class-style workouts fit how you like to train.</p>
 <h3>MyFitnessPal</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> detailed food, calorie and macronutrient logging with exercise and partner-app connections.</p><p><strong>Consider:</strong> logging is a tool, not a complete training program. Detailed tracking can be useful for some people and unnecessarily consuming for others.</p>
 <h3>Nike Training Club</h3><p><strong>Best for:</strong> free trainer-led strength, conditioning, yoga, mobility and home or gym workouts.</p><p><strong>Consider:</strong> nutrition is primarily educational guidance rather than the central tracking function.</p>
 <h3>Everyone’s an Athlete</h3><p><strong>Status:</strong> coming soon. The planned approach connects guided training with body care, mental performance and practical nutrition and wellness education.</p><p><strong>Consider:</strong> it should not be treated as a finished-app recommendation until the product is publicly available and its final features can be tested.</p>
 <h2 id="choose">How to choose the right app</h2>
 <ol><li><strong>Name the primary problem.</strong> Do you need workout structure, food awareness, meal ideas, recovery support or fewer disconnected apps?</li><li><strong>Choose the nutrition style.</strong> Decide between education, recipes, meal planning or detailed food logging.</li><li><strong>Check workout depth.</strong> A list of exercises is different from a progressive training program.</li><li><strong>Review the free and paid tiers.</strong> Confirm which features require a subscription and how cancellation works.</li><li><strong>Examine privacy.</strong> Understand what health, food and activity data are collected and shared.</li><li><strong>Test the workflow.</strong> The app should make healthy actions easier, not turn the day into constant data entry.</li></ol>
 <h2>Nutrition education vs. food tracking</h2>
 <div class="grid two">
  <div class="card"><h3>Education</h3><p>Helps users understand balanced meals, protein, hydration, recovery and decision-making without recording every item.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>Tracking</h3><p>Creates more detailed awareness of intake and nutrients but requires time and can create false precision when entries are inaccurate.</p></div>
 </div>
 <p>Neither approach is universally superior. Some people benefit from a short period of logging; others do better with meal structure and behavior-focused guidance.</p>
 <h2 id="safety">Use nutrition tracking in a healthy way</h2>
 <ul><li>Do not treat estimated calorie numbers as exact.</li><li>Choose goals that support health and training rather than punishment.</li><li>Take a break if logging increases guilt, anxiety or rigid eating.</li><li>Use professional guidance for eating disorders, medical conditions, pregnancy or complex nutrition needs.</li><li>Remember that sleep, stress, access to food and culture also shape eating habits.</li></ul>
 <div class="callout"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> choose Centr or FitOn for an integrated workout-and-meal experience, MyFitnessPal for detailed logging, and Nike Training Club for a strong workout-first free option. Watch Everyone’s an Athlete as it develops a connected training, recovery, mindset and nutrition approach.</div>
